On 29 Jun 2015, at 16:39, Henry G <henrym...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Good day,
>
> I would like to ask you if the Complex Region Spatial Smoother (CReSS) method can be implemented with INLA?

Hi,
having had a brief look at the Scott-Hayward et al 2011 preprint at
https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/bitstream/10023/2048/1/CReSS_manuscript_unblinded.pdf
(I don't have access to the 2014 paper today)
my answer is "possibly, but it doesn't look trivial", unless it can be rewritten on a form that doesn't explicitly require pre-calculation of the basis functions.

The complex region smoothing method you get by constructing a complex mesh in R-INLA and applying a model including an spde over that mesh results in a method closer to both the Ramsay and the Wood approaches.  The soap film smoother can in principle be implemented in inla by modifying the spde models, but to my knowledge no one has yet done so. Similarly for the Ramsay methods, which in some cases (partially depending how the mesh is chosen, I think) are equivalent to the SPDE methods.

>
